1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,331
Hello. My name is Lusani Mudau, student number

2
00:00:04,331 --> 00:00:08,642
ST10474461, and this is Part 3 of my RaceDay

3
00:00:08,642 --> 00:00:11,037
portfolio of evidence for PROG6222.

4
00:00:11,037 --> 00:00:14,869
Part 1 planned the system and designed the

5
00:00:14,869 --> 00:00:18,222
database. Part 2 built the REST API.

6
00:00:18,222 --> 00:00:22,532
Part 3, this video, is the MVC web application

7
00:00:22,532 --> 00:00:24,448
that consumes that API.

8
00:00:24,448 --> 00:00:27,801
Sign-in and registration are the only public

9
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:31,154
pages. A new organiser account also needs

10
00:00:31,154 --> 00:00:33,070
the access code, RACEDAY-ORG-2026.

11
00:00:33,070 --> 00:00:36,805
From here, every request follows one path.

12
00:00:36,805 --> 00:00:39,698
The browser talks to the MVC application,

13
00:00:39,698 --> 00:00:43,048
the MVC application talks to the API,

14
00:00:43,048 --> 00:00:46,960
and only the API talks to SQL Server.

15
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:50,313
The home page is the participant landing

16
00:00:50,313 --> 00:00:53,666
page. The upcoming events and their banners

17
00:00:53,666 --> 00:00:56,540
are all served by the API.

18
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:01,505
Here is the solution in Visual Studio.

19
00:01:01,505 --> 00:01:03,600
It contains three projects.

20
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:07,790
RaceDay.Api is the REST API from Part 2.

21
00:01:07,790 --> 00:01:11,706
RaceDay.Mvc is the web application in this

22
00:01:11,706 --> 00:01:14,074
video. RaceDay.Api.test holds the automated

23
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:18,263
tests, eighty-five of them, and all of them

24
00:01:18,263 --> 00:01:19,537
pass.

25
00:01:19,537 --> 00:01:22,453
The MVC project follows the normal ASP.NET

26
00:01:22,453 --> 00:01:24,587
Core layout. Controllers handle requests.

27
00:01:24,587 --> 00:01:28,213
Views and ViewModels build the interface.

28
00:01:28,213 --> 00:01:31,356
Services contain the API client. wwwroot

29
00:01:31,356 --> 00:01:33,450
holds the static assets.

30
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:37,640
The important detail is what the MVC project

31
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:41,219
does not contain. Open the project file and

32
00:01:41,219 --> 00:01:45,499
there are no NuGet packages at all.

33
00:01:45,499 --> 00:01:48,637
No Entity Framework, no SQL client,

34
00:01:48,637 --> 00:01:50,762
no Azure SDK.

35
00:01:50,762 --> 00:01:54,921
If I search the whole MVC project for DbContext,

36
00:01:54,921 --> 00:01:57,016
there are zero results.

37
00:01:57,016 --> 00:02:00,682
The web application has no database connection

38
00:02:00,682 --> 00:02:05,525
string and no way to reach the database directly.

39
00:02:05,525 --> 00:02:09,061
That is deliberate. Every read and every

40
00:02:09,061 --> 00:02:11,680
write goes through the API.

41
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:16,455
All API calls live in one typed client,

42
00:02:16,455 --> 00:02:19,796
RaceDayApiClient. Each method maps to exactly

43
00:02:19,796 --> 00:02:21,257
one API endpoint.

44
00:02:21,257 --> 00:02:25,152
There is no business logic here. The client

45
00:02:25,152 --> 00:02:27,921
sends a request, receives JSON, and hands

46
00:02:27,921 --> 00:02:31,176
it back to the controller.

47
00:02:31,176 --> 00:02:35,390
The interesting part is how the API session

48
00:02:35,390 --> 00:02:38,415
is carried. The API issues its own session

49
00:02:38,415 --> 00:02:41,221
cookie, called RaceDay.Session. The browser

50
00:02:41,221 --> 00:02:45,116
never sees that cookie. It is kept server-side

51
00:02:45,116 --> 00:02:46,133
in the MVC session, separately for each signed-in

52
00:02:46,133 --> 00:02:48,813
user.

53
00:02:48,813 --> 00:02:52,420
These lines capture the Set-Cookie header

54
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:55,341
after every response. These lines attach

55
00:02:55,341 --> 00:02:59,106
it to the next request and handle an unauthorised

56
00:02:59,106 --> 00:03:00,211
response.

57
00:03:00,211 --> 00:03:03,990
The HTTP handler is created with cookie use

58
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:08,001
switched off, so it keeps no shared cookie

59
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:11,410
jar. That guarantees one user API session

60
00:03:11,410 --> 00:03:14,818
can never leak into another user request.

61
00:03:14,818 --> 00:03:19,265
If the API returns 401, the client throws

62
00:03:19,265 --> 00:03:21,649
an ApiUnauthorizedException. A global exception

63
00:03:21,649 --> 00:03:25,554
filter catches it, signs the MVC user out,

64
00:03:25,554 --> 00:03:27,888
clears the stored API cookie, and redirects

65
00:03:27,888 --> 00:03:30,400
to the login page.

66
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:35,192
The MVC application has its own cookie authentication,

67
00:03:35,192 --> 00:03:37,657
separate from the API session.

68
00:03:37,657 --> 00:03:41,573
Signing in posts the credentials to the API,

69
00:03:41,573 --> 00:03:45,786
and the app keeps a local cookie containing

70
00:03:45,786 --> 00:03:48,215
the user name and role.

71
00:03:48,215 --> 00:03:51,616
I will sign in as a participant.

72
00:03:51,616 --> 00:03:55,442
The navigation is rendered from that role:

73
00:03:55,442 --> 00:03:58,144
Home, Events, My enrolments, My results.

74
00:03:58,144 --> 00:04:00,282
There is no organiser menu.

75
00:04:00,282 --> 00:04:04,731
Now I will sign out and sign in as

76
00:04:04,731 --> 00:04:05,703
the organiser.

77
00:04:05,703 --> 00:04:09,702
The menu changes to Dashboard, My events

78
00:04:09,702 --> 00:04:10,560
and Browse events.

79
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:13,142
Every organiser controller also carries an

80
00:04:13,142 --> 00:04:16,628
authorise attribute for the Organiser role,

81
00:04:16,628 --> 00:04:19,790
so the server enforces this as well.

82
00:04:21,090 --> 00:04:24,875
The Events page lists every event and supports

83
00:04:24,875 --> 00:04:28,082
search and filtering by event type.

84
00:04:28,082 --> 00:04:30,721
Opening an event shows the banner,

85
00:04:30,721 --> 00:04:33,886
the date, the location, the distance,

86
00:04:33,886 --> 00:04:36,824
and every category with its price.

87
00:04:36,824 --> 00:04:39,545
I will enter the Johannesburg Sunrise Walk,

88
00:04:39,545 --> 00:04:42,385
in the five kilometre walk category.

89
00:04:42,385 --> 00:04:45,697
That entry now appears on My enrolments,

90
00:04:45,697 --> 00:04:48,544
with colour-coded statuses. Confirmed is

91
00:04:48,544 --> 00:04:50,241
green, Pending is amber, and Cancelled is

92
00:04:50,241 --> 00:04:51,713
red.

93
00:04:51,713 --> 00:04:54,688
This participant already has all three,

94
00:04:54,688 --> 00:04:58,001
so the states are visible at once.

95
00:04:58,001 --> 00:05:02,260
The badge class is chosen in code from the

96
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:06,046
status value, and the same component is reused

97
00:05:06,046 --> 00:05:07,831
on the organiser screen.

98
00:05:07,831 --> 00:05:11,589
My results is the page that proves the results

99
00:05:11,589 --> 00:05:15,037
feature. This participant finished the Pretoria

100
00:05:15,037 --> 00:05:18,327
Capital Marathon in two hours, thirty-six

101
00:05:18,327 --> 00:05:19,879
minutes and fifty seconds.

102
00:05:19,879 --> 00:05:22,383
The page reads forty-seventh of three hundred

103
00:05:22,383 --> 00:05:26,394
and twelve finishers, with the ordinal suffix

104
00:05:26,394 --> 00:05:29,908
generated in code, so it says 47th and not

105
00:05:29,908 --> 00:05:31,126
47.

106
00:05:31,126 --> 00:05:34,358
Finally, the profile page. The personal details

107
00:05:34,358 --> 00:05:38,698
come from the API, and the picture is uploaded

108
00:05:38,698 --> 00:05:42,385
as a file to the API upload endpoint.

109
00:05:42,385 --> 00:05:46,270
The URL that comes back is displayed here.

110
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:51,095
Signing in as the organiser opens the dashboard:

111
00:05:51,095 --> 00:05:55,724
five events, four upcoming, and four hundred

112
00:05:55,724 --> 00:05:57,997
and thirty-three enrolments. Every number

113
00:05:57,997 --> 00:05:59,605
comes from the API.

114
00:05:59,605 --> 00:06:04,118
I will create a new event. First I submit

115
00:06:04,118 --> 00:06:07,684
the form empty. Instead of an error page,

116
00:06:07,684 --> 00:06:11,546
the form returns with inline validation messages,

117
00:06:11,546 --> 00:06:14,974
and it keeps the values I already typed.

118
00:06:14,974 --> 00:06:18,774
That is the graceful validation requirement.

119
00:06:18,774 --> 00:06:22,851
Now I fill it in and attach a banner

120
00:06:22,851 --> 00:06:27,109
image. The event is created through the API,

121
00:06:27,109 --> 00:06:31,882
and the banner goes through the API dedicated

122
00:06:31,882 --> 00:06:34,708
banner endpoint. The returned URL is displayed

123
00:06:34,708 --> 00:06:37,079
on the card immediately.

124
00:06:37,079 --> 00:06:40,726
From My events I can manage everything for

125
00:06:40,726 --> 00:06:44,737
that event. Under Categories, I add a category

126
00:06:44,737 --> 00:06:48,519
with its distance and price, and the table

127
00:06:48,519 --> 00:06:49,251
updates.

128
00:06:49,251 --> 00:06:52,259
Under Enrolments, every participant is listed

129
00:06:52,259 --> 00:06:56,440
with their status. This view is read-only

130
00:06:56,440 --> 00:06:58,829
for the organiser, because entries change

131
00:06:58,829 --> 00:07:02,250
when a participant enrols or cancels.

132
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:04,697
Under Results, the Pretoria Marathon already

133
00:07:04,697 --> 00:07:08,306
has three hundred and twelve captured results,

134
00:07:08,306 --> 00:07:12,820
each shown as a position out of the total

135
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:16,077
finishers.

136
00:07:16,077 --> 00:07:17,834
For an event with no results yet, the page

137
00:07:17,834 --> 00:07:20,843
lists the confirmed participants who are

138
00:07:20,843 --> 00:07:24,855
still waiting. I capture one, with a finish

139
00:07:24,855 --> 00:07:29,037
time and a position. It is saved through

140
00:07:29,037 --> 00:07:32,783
the API and appears in the table immediately.

141
00:07:32,783 --> 00:07:35,887
Every write action, for events, categories,

142
00:07:35,887 --> 00:07:39,525
enrolments and results, goes through the

143
00:07:39,525 --> 00:07:42,407
API. The MVC application never writes to

144
00:07:42,407 --> 00:07:43,410
the database.

145
00:07:44,710 --> 00:07:48,596
Image uploads go through the API as well.

146
00:07:48,596 --> 00:07:51,962
Storage sits behind an interface called IFileStorage,

147
00:07:51,962 --> 00:07:53,956
with two implementations, and FileStorageRegistration

148
00:07:53,956 --> 00:07:57,341
chooses between them once at startup.

149
00:07:57,341 --> 00:08:00,742
When the provider is set to Azure,

150
00:08:00,742 --> 00:08:02,663
it registers AzureBlobFileStorage.

151
00:08:02,663 --> 00:08:05,114
Otherwise it registers the local provider

152
00:08:05,114 --> 00:08:09,000
that writes into wwwroot. The rest of the

153
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,059
application only depends on the interface.

154
00:08:12,059 --> 00:08:14,344
AzureBlobFileStorage uses a blob container

155
00:08:14,344 --> 00:08:18,231
client. It creates the container if it does

156
00:08:18,231 --> 00:08:22,118
not exist, uploads the stream with a content

157
00:08:22,118 --> 00:08:26,004
type and a generated name, and returns the

158
00:08:26,004 --> 00:08:29,198
public URL. Deleting an image removes the

159
00:08:29,198 --> 00:08:31,648
blob.

160
00:08:31,648 --> 00:08:32,806
To demonstrate this honestly, without an

161
00:08:32,806 --> 00:08:36,206
Azure subscription, I am running the Azurite

162
00:08:36,206 --> 00:08:38,494
storage emulator, which implements the same

163
00:08:38,494 --> 00:08:43,512
Blob API. The provider is set to Azure and

164
00:08:43,512 --> 00:08:46,408
the connection string points at Azurite.

165
00:08:46,408 --> 00:08:49,951
Uploading a banner now writes a real blob,

166
00:08:49,951 --> 00:08:54,182
and the API serves that URL straight back

167
00:08:54,182 --> 00:08:57,920
to the MVC application. None of this storage

168
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:02,440
code is in the MVC project. The web application

169
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:04,870
only ever receives a URL.

170
00:09:06,170 --> 00:09:10,066
The whole system runs in Docker. The API

171
00:09:10,066 --> 00:09:14,449
and the MVC app each have a multi-stage Dockerfile:

172
00:09:14,449 --> 00:09:17,859
restore and publish in the SDK image,

173
00:09:17,859 --> 00:09:20,902
then copy only the published output into

174
00:09:20,902 --> 00:09:23,216
the small runtime image.

175
00:09:23,216 --> 00:09:25,652
Docker Compose defines three services:

176
00:09:25,652 --> 00:09:29,835
SQL Server on 1433, the API on 8080,

177
00:09:29,835 --> 00:09:31,983
and the MVC application on 8081.

178
00:09:31,983 --> 00:09:35,393
The connection string, the organiser access

179
00:09:35,393 --> 00:09:39,592
code and the storage provider all come from

180
00:09:39,592 --> 00:09:42,334
environment variables in the Compose file,

181
00:09:42,334 --> 00:09:46,408
so the same images can run against a different

182
00:09:46,408 --> 00:09:49,959
database, or a real Azure account,

183
00:09:49,959 --> 00:09:50,978
without a rebuild.

184
00:09:50,978 --> 00:09:53,901
One command starts everything: docker compose

185
00:09:53,901 --> 00:09:57,797
up, with the build flag. The API applies

186
00:09:57,797 --> 00:10:01,694
its migrations at startup, and the MVC app

187
00:10:01,694 --> 00:10:05,103
reaches the API through the service name

188
00:10:05,103 --> 00:10:09,486
on the Compose network, so there are no hard-coded

189
00:10:09,486 --> 00:10:10,460
host addresses.

190
00:10:10,460 --> 00:10:13,383
Here are the three containers running,

191
00:10:13,383 --> 00:10:17,279
the MVC app answering on 8081, and Swagger

192
00:10:17,279 --> 00:10:19,123
documenting the API on 8080.

193
00:10:19,123 --> 00:10:22,637
The pipeline is in GitHub Actions.

194
00:10:22,637 --> 00:10:27,169
On every push it restores and builds the

195
00:10:27,169 --> 00:10:29,962
whole solution in Release, runs the eighty-five

196
00:10:29,962 --> 00:10:33,352
tests, publishes the MVC project, and uploads

197
00:10:33,352 --> 00:10:36,424
the publish output as an artifact.

198
00:10:36,424 --> 00:10:38,710
The latest run is green.

199
00:10:40,010 --> 00:10:45,040
That is Part 3: an MVC application that consumes

200
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:48,670
the Part 2 API, with session-based authentication,

201
00:10:48,670 --> 00:10:50,835
role-based navigation, complete participant

202
00:10:50,835 --> 00:10:54,083
and organiser journeys, Azure Blob Storage

203
00:10:54,083 --> 00:10:59,610
behind the API, and a Docker and CI pipeline.

204
00:10:59,610 --> 00:11:01,120
Thank you for watching.
